# BG + EN for every user-facing string

Veversal ships **English and Bulgarian** for all user-visible copy. Do not merge hardcoded or single-language strings.

## How to translate

- Import **`useTranslation`** from `src/i18n` and use **`t('dotted.key')`** for every new/changed user-facing string in components, screens, and hooks.
- Add or update the **same key** in both files in the same PR/commit:
  - `src/i18n/en.json`
  - `src/i18n/bg.json`
- Included: buttons, labels, placeholders, helper text, empty states, status labels, alerts/toasts, errors, and navigation chrome.

## API-sourced names

- Catalog values (RepairType, fuel type, etc.): use **`translateRepairTypeLabel`**, **`translateFuelTypeLabel`**, or other localized fields when available.
- If the API has no localized name, still i18n all **chrome** around it; note follow-up in the PR if catalog data needs backend localization.

## Done checklist

- Keys exist in **en** and **bg** with matching structure.
- Run the app with locale **BG** on touched screens — no English chrome left on new UI.

## Examples

```jsx
// Bad
<Text>Save changes</Text>

// Good
const { t } = useTranslation();
<Text>{t('common.saveChanges')}</Text>
```

Backend API messages that surface to users: coordinate gettext in `vhr` `locale/en/` and `locale/bg/` when changing server copy.
